{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790254154,"nonce":"1Jrh04TgR39HRRwKevvVv1sFSu4=","title":"Examples flakes on x86-64: JIT call displacement overflows (compiled_blob.rs:142)","message":"Examples (Linux x86-64) fails intermittently, most recently run 35555226155 (2026-09-21, 785dee7e), during test_haxe_stdlib after std_int:\n\n    cranelift-jit-0.135.1/src/compiled_blob.rs:142:80:\n    called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: TryFromIntError(PosOverflow)\n\nb58af6ad diagnosed it: a direct call between two JIT-defined functions of one module is encoded as X86CallPCRel4, and cranelift-jit has no out-of-range fallback on x86-64 (aarch64 gets veneers). The default SystemMemoryProvider maps each allocation independently, so whether two allocations land within 2GB depends on mmap placement; re-running the same commit passes.\n\nFix: on x86-64 give the JITBuilder a provider that carves every allocation from one reserved ArenaMemoryProvider region (1GB reserved, PROT_NONE until used; 64MB on Windows, which commits the reservation), falling back to SystemMemoryProvider only when the arena is exhausted. A unit test checks allocations of every kind stay within i32 reach.","files":null}]}